\section{Methodological details}{

\strong{Methods and method selection}
\itemize{
\item \strong{Original publication}: Benjamini, Krieger & Yekutieli (2006), the
adaptive two-stage extension of the original BH procedure.
\item \strong{Main references}: Benjamini, Krieger & Yekutieli (2006) for the
procedure itself; Benjamini & Hochberg (1995) for the underlying FDR
framework it adapts. Full citations appear under "References" below.
\item \strong{Typical applications}: correcting for multiple testing when a lot
of real signal is expected to be present (the common case in
gridded environmental data) and the extra statistical power over
\code{\link[=fdr_bh]{fdr_bh()}}'s fixed threshold is worth the added complexity -- see
\code{\link[=workflow_tst]{workflow_tst()}} for a workflow that defaults to this method for
that reason.
}

\strong{Statistical assumptions}

BKY is adaptive, not a safeguard against arbitrary dependence. Its
FDR interpretation requires the assumptions of the selected two-stage
procedure; estimating \code{pi0} does not itself remove dependence among
tests. Use \code{\link[=fdr_by]{fdr_by()}} when an arbitrary-dependence guarantee is needed.

\strong{Computational considerations}

Both implementations are dominated by ordering or BH adjustment of
the valid p-values and are lightweight relative to raster trend tests.

\strong{Limitations}

The \code{"multtest"} and \code{"original"} branches are genuine but distinct
second-stage conventions. Under \code{"original"}, the function returns a
rejection decision but \code{q_value} is \code{NA}, because that branch does not
define the monotone adjusted p-values returned by the rescaling branch.

\strong{Quality assurance}

The default branch is validated against the behaviour reached through
\code{cp4p} and \code{multtest}; the literal-paper branch is checked against
direct step-up calculations. Automated tests cover missing values,
degenerate stages, monotonicity, thresholds, and returned diagnostics.
}

